WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:01.501
-Our next guest is

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.169
an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:03.869
you know from shows such as

00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:04.804
"Friday Night Lights,"

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.105
"Fargo," and "Breaking Bad."

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:08.875
He stars in the new film
"The Power of the Dog,"

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:11.778
which is in select theaters now
and on Netflix December 1st.

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:12.912
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:15.648
-Forward, 2, 3.

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.717
Side, 2, hm.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.953
[ Humming ]

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:28.962
-[ Crying ]

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:34.434
-What is it, George?

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:42.641
-Oh, just --

00:00:42.641 --> 00:00:43.576
[ Sigh ]

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:49.382
I wanted to say how nice it is

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:51.417
not to be alone.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.286
-Please welcome back to the show
Jesse Plemons, everyone.

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:57.423
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:57.423 --> 00:00:59.992
♪♪

00:00:59.992 --> 00:01:01.327
[ Whistling ]
♪♪

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:07.366
♪♪

00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:11.504
♪♪

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.139
Welcome back!
-Thank you.

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:16.442
It's a good segue from Samberg,
that clip.

00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:17.977
-The end of that clip, yeah.
It had a real

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:19.812
Samberg vibe to it.
-Yeah, it did. It did.

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:21.681
[ Laughter ]
-I remember I heard the casting

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:23.249
came down to you or him.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.517
-It did.
It was very close.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.818
[ Laughter ]

00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:27.353
-Because he read
that line like --

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:29.055
[ As Samberg ]
It's nice to be with people!

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:31.824
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:33.025
We were talking backstage.
That's your fiancée,

00:01:33.025 --> 00:01:34.360
Kirsten Dunst, in the clip.
-That is, yeah.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.997
-And you were saying
it's like cheating to get to --

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:39.198
you guys get to work together.

00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:41.200
-It doesn't seem fair, yeah.
-No.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.903
You got to be in New Zealand
and work with your fiancée.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:45.705
-Yeah. You know, I mean,
that was the second time

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:48.374
we had worked together
and it just, you know,

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:51.444
all of the sort of hard work
is already done for it.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:55.081 line:0%
You just kind of have
to figure out how to, you know,

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:58.284 line:0%
deal with each other and the
specific characters and --

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.519
-It's really nice.
-Yeah.

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:01.621
-And then,
outside of working together,

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:04.256
you guys added to your family.

00:02:04.256 --> 00:02:07.793
You had a baby a few --
What, seven months old, now?

00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:11.197
-Yeah, a seven-month-old baby
that we decided, "Why not?

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.266
Let's have a pandemic baby."
-Yeah.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:15.935
[ Laughter ]
You get to spend time together.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:17.570
-Yeah.
-And you got your older one.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.305
Is Ennis the older one?

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.841
-Ennis is the older one.
He's three and a half.

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:26.145
Did you experience
any sort of --

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:29.682
any problems with the older
child dealing with the new?

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:33.218
-There was one time where,
because we had a two-year gap

00:02:33.218 --> 00:02:35.488
where the two-year-old walked
across the room

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.123
and the baby was on the floor

00:02:37.123 --> 00:02:39.625
and he just fully stepped
on top of it.

00:02:39.625 --> 00:02:40.693
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, wow.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.027
-And like with a big --

00:02:42.027 --> 00:02:43.362
And then,
he said it was an accident,

00:02:43.362 --> 00:02:46.799
but it was on like the Nest Cam,
you know, like the --

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:48.100
[ Laughter ]
and you he just had the most

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.436
[bleep]-eating grin, like --
-Oh, wow.

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:51.537
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:53.639
-Accidentally.
-Did you have anything?

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:56.475
-Well, we kept waiting
for the other shoe to drop

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:58.378
because you hear
these terrible stories

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:02.115
of the older kid, you know,
reacting really awfully.

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:05.818
And, one night,
we were in our room

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.520
and our kid runs in

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.056
and we have the bassinet
next to our bed

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:12.891
and he just says,
"I'm going to pee on the floor."

00:03:12.891 --> 00:03:14.126
[ Laughter ]
We're like, "No, no, no, no.

00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:15.328
You can't pee on the floor."
He's like --

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:17.830
And I don't think he really had
to pee that bad.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.300
It was just to prove a point
and mark his territory.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.134
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.134 --> 00:03:24.937
And, sure enough, he did,

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:27.106
as we were pleading
with him not to.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.542
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:30.543
And that was kind of it.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.311
It was like,
once he had done that...

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:33.112
-Wow!
-...he was like,

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:35.248
"Alright, my work is done."

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:36.983
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:38.617
You get it.

00:03:38.617 --> 00:03:40.453
-At seven months, I feel like,
at this point,

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:41.720
there's a personality, right?

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.723
Like what do you call the baby?

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.225
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:47.459
-We call him Big Kahuna.

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:48.695
[ Laughter ]
-Big Kahuna?

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:50.095
-Big Kahuna.

00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:51.964
It's very fitting,
on many levels.

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:55.735
He's in the 96th percentile
in weight, so there's that.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:04:00.173
But he also just has a very
laid-back, easygoing vibe.

00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:02.809
He's kind of like
a good old boy already

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:04.610
in like a seven-month-old
package, yeah.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.278
[ Laughter ]
-The Big Kahuna?

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:08.214
I love the Big Kahuna.

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:10.816
The first time you were here --
speaking of family --

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:12.652
your grandmother was
in the audience.

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:14.119
-Yes.
-And, the next time,

00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:16.088
it was weird because I got
to meet her, which was lovely,

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:17.690
and then,
the next time you came,

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.058
you said she sent
a big Texas howdy,

00:04:20.058 --> 00:04:21.293
which was very sweet.

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:22.495
Did we hear from her?

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:24.063
-We did hear from her.
-Okay.

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:26.966
-And she wanted me to make sure

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:28.968
that I relayed this message
to you.

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:29.836
-Okay.

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:30.669
-"Mr. Meyers..."

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:32.004
-Very nice of her to use.

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:33.873
[ Laughter ]
-She's much more formal in text

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.408
than she is
when she's just talking.

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:37.142
-Right.
-She usually calls you old Seth.

00:04:37.142 --> 00:04:38.810
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.980
-"Mr. Meyers, just a big
Texas hello from Jesse's granny

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:43.349
and to wish you and your family

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:45.752
a blessed and wonderful
Thanksgiving," so.

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:47.153
[ Audience awws ]
I mean, she's just A1.

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:48.788
-Yeah.
-I got no complaints

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:49.688
about her at all.

00:04:49.688 --> 00:04:50.890
Consistently,

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.358
of all my guests on the show,

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.693
she's my favorite grandma.
-She's the best granny, yeah.

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:55.227
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:55.227 --> 00:04:57.696
-Do you see her
for Thanksgiving?

00:04:57.696 --> 00:05:00.065
-Pretty much most holidays
when we're in Texas.

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:01.767
We alternate now because of,
you know.

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:03.002
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:06.072
-And so do you have
big Thanksgiving plans?

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.307
-We do.
Yeah, we've got the whole --

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:09.642
We've got the whole family,

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:12.144
Kirsten's family coming
into Austin

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:14.814
and then, for Christmas,
we'll do it all again, yeah.

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:16.381
-That's great.

00:05:16.381 --> 00:05:17.817
This is a beautiful film.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:19.718
Benedict Cumberbatch was here,
talking about it

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:20.953
and we've talked in the past.

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:22.688
You're a lovely person
in real life.

00:05:22.688 --> 00:05:24.890
You've played some pretty good
sociopaths over the years.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:26.425
[ Laughter ]
-Thank you.
-Which I think speaks

00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:27.760
to your acting style,
that, you know,

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.695
I meet you
and there's an actual light

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:31.497
in your eyes, as opposed to some
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.765
of the people you've played.
-I know how to turn it off.

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:35.401
[ Laughter ]
-You know how to turn it off?

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:38.270
You are the nicer of the two
brothers in this film.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:40.940
Did you enjoy being
on the other side of it?

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.675
-It was so nice.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.978
It was like a vacation
from playing socio--

00:05:45.978 --> 00:05:48.380
playing, you know,
the heavy for a while.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:51.316
And, yeah, just to get to work
with Benedict as well

00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:53.986
and to see him do something that
he'd never done before.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.688
-He told us that he didn't --

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.756
that Jane Campion,
the director of this film,

00:05:57.756 --> 00:06:01.727
asked him not to shower,
so that you could all know

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:04.697
what somebody like that
would smell like.

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:05.698
[ Laughter ]

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:09.068
-Yeah, that was one
of the requests.

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:12.738
And, you know, I never really --

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:17.610
I can't say that I noticed,
but we did do some --

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.445
I think we discussed earlier
we did some sort of

00:06:20.445 --> 00:06:23.649
unorthodox rehearsals,

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:26.419
where like the first thing
that we did together was

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:30.456
we waltzed
to this Elliott Smith song.

00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:32.158
Like brothers do, you know?

00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:34.126
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.126 --> 00:06:37.497
And that was sort of her way
of getting us very familiar

00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:40.933
with each other's center,
physicality or --

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:42.501
[ Laughter ]
-I love it.
-And, you know.

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:43.502
-It worked.
-I guess it worked.

00:06:43.502 --> 00:06:44.870
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.138
-The whole time you're watching
the film, you're like,

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:47.406
"Those guys know
what each other smells like."

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:49.475
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:52.578
Doing a Western like this,

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:53.979
were Westerns
something you were into

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:57.082
and you hope to pass that
down to your boys?

00:06:57.082 --> 00:06:59.785
-I do. I do hope so.
I grew up watching

00:06:59.785 --> 00:07:02.788
a film called "Lonesome Dove."
-With Tommy Lee Jones.

00:07:02.788 --> 00:07:04.156
The best.
-Tommy Lee Jones.

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:05.591
-Have you seen it?
-It's the best, yeah.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.726
-Okay.

00:07:07.726 --> 00:07:11.096
I mean, I did try
to show my son the film,

00:07:11.096 --> 00:07:12.498
but it is six hours long.

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:15.067
-It's six hours long
and it's super brutal.

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:17.235
-It's super brutal.
My parents thought it was okay

00:07:17.235 --> 00:07:20.472
for me to start watching that
at like one year old,

00:07:20.472 --> 00:07:22.375
[ Laughter ]
so I guess I was like,

00:07:22.375 --> 00:07:24.109
"Well, if it's good enough
for me..."

00:07:24.109 --> 00:07:25.444
-You waited 'til he was
three and a half.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:27.446
-Yeah.
He wasn't into it.

00:07:27.446 --> 00:07:28.547
He wasn't into "Lonesome Dove."
-Yeah.

00:07:28.547 --> 00:07:29.982
-It does seem like
a little advanced

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:30.883
for three and a half, yeah.

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:33.785
[ Laughter ]
-Not for me, but.

00:07:33.785 --> 00:07:35.588
I just came out --
-You turned out fine.

00:07:35.588 --> 00:07:37.089
-Yeah, right?
-Yeah. Well, tell him

00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:38.991
to toughen up.
You know who's going to like it?

00:07:38.991 --> 00:07:41.794
The Big Kahuna.
-Big Kahuna's going to love it.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:43.462
-Hey, congratulations
on everything.

00:07:43.462 --> 00:07:44.764
It's so nice to see you.
-Thank you so much.

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:45.832
-Thank you so much
for making time for us.

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:47.165
[ Cheering and applause ]
Jesse Plemons.

00:07:47.165 --> 00:07:49.368
"The Power of the Dog" is out
in select theaters now

00:07:49.368 --> 00:07:50.903
and available on Netflix
December 1st.

